SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','A hundredth is 1/100. Therefore 152 hundredths is 152 ÷ 100 = 1.52.'),
2:('DIG-01','Do the multiplications before adding. The expression becomes 1 + 10 + 100 + 1000 = 1111.'),
3:('FRAC-02','Five balls cost 250 cents, so one costs 250 ÷ 5 = 50 cents. Three cost 3 × 50 = 150 cents, or $1.50.'),
4:('RATE-01','Auckland is ahead, so add two hours to Melbourne’s time. Two hours after 2 am is 4 am.'),
5:('COUNT-06','Count full-height rectangles first. Four vertical boundary lines give six pairs, hence six full-height rectangles. The extra horizontal segments make two half-height rectangles at the left and two at the right. They do not continue through the middle. Total: 6 + 2 + 2 = 10.'),
6:('FRAC-03','The sale price is 75% of the original. If 75% costs $6, then each 25% costs $2. That missing 25% is the saving: $2. Check: original $8 minus $2 equals $6.'),
7:('SOLID-03','Try folding D with its bottom middle square as the base. The left and right squares rise as opposite walls. The two squares above them both fold into the same remaining wall position, so they overlap instead of giving four different walls. D cannot form the open box.'),
8:('DIV-04','An even number contains a factor of 2. Multiplying it by an odd integer keeps that factor, so the product is even. This proves D. For comparison, odd + odd is even, odd × odd is odd, and odd + even is odd.'),
9:('DATA-03','Read the graph at the two specified times: 9 am is 1°C and 11 am is −2°C. The difference is 1 − (−2) = 3°C.'),
10:('DATA-02','Divide the total allowed mass by the number of people: 1000 ÷ 13 is about 76.9 kg per person. Of the offered values, 80 kg is closest.'),
11:('ALG-01','Test the earnings using $5 per mow and $9 per wash. Four of each gives 4 × 5 + 4 × 9 = 20 + 36 = $56, exactly the stated amount. The other options give $52, $57, $55 and $61.'),
12:('GEO-02','The whole square has area 8 × 8 = 64 cm². The shaded small tangram triangle occupies 1/16 of the square. Equivalently, its horizontal base is 4 cm and its height is 2 cm, so its area is ½ × 4 × 2 = 4 cm².'),
13:('DATA-01','Split the regular hexagon into six equal triangles from its centre. The region labelled 2 is one of those six triangles, so its chance is 1/6. Over 72 spins the expected count is 72 ÷ 6 = 12. An actual experiment need not give exactly 12.'),
14:('SPATIAL-01','A house east and a tree west require the house and tree to share your row. Row 10 has the tree at D10 and house at L10. The person directly north is at J12, fixing your column as J. You are at J10.'),
15:('FRAC-01','At the second visit to the marker, Jim has run one whole lap plus one-third of a lap: 4/3 laps. The whole race is two laps, so the completed fraction is (4/3) ÷ 2 = 2/3.'),
16:('ALG-01','Let each twin be t years old. Graham is t + 3 and Bill is t + 5. Their combined age is 4t + 8 = 40, so t = 8. Graham is 8 + 3 = 11. Check: 8 + 8 + 11 + 13 = 40.'),
17:('DIV-02','Lines that run all the way between opposite sides make a square grid. Use the largest square size that divides both 56 and 98: their greatest common divisor is 14. This makes 4 rows and 7 columns, requiring 3 horizontal and 6 vertical internal lines. Total: 9 lines.'),
18:('SOLID-01','Let the prism’s dimensions be a, b and c. Multiplying the face areas gives (ab)(bc)(ac) = (abc)² = 12 × 15 × 20 = 3600. The positive square root is 60, so the volume is 60 cm³. The dimensions 3, 4 and 5 provide a direct check.'),
19:('GEO-05','Each fold halves one dimension. Distribute the three folds between the two dimensions. The possible final rectangles are 1 by 12, 2 by 6, 4 by 3, and 8 by 1.5 cm. Their perimeters are 26, 16, 14 and 19 cm. The smallest is 14 cm, from 4 by 3.'),
20:('LOGIC-02','The visible faces show 28, 34 and 36. Six consecutive even numbers containing all three can only be 26–36 or 28–38. In the first set, opposite pairs would sum to 62, making 28 and 34 opposite; the picture shows them adjacent, so that is impossible. The set must be 28, 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, whose sum is 198.'),
21:('DIG-05','In the units column, the missing digit plus 7 ends in 2, so that digit is 5 and there is a carry of 1. The tens column gives 9 + 8 + 1 = 18, so the missing tens digit is 8 with a carry of 1. The two missing hundreds digits must sum to 9, producing 10 with the carry and a leading 1. The missing digits therefore total 5 + 8 + 9 + 1 = 23, regardless of the order of the hundreds digits.'),
22:('SPATIAL-01','A shortest route is P–U–Q–U–R–S–T, with length 4 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 3 = 20 km. Revisiting U is allowed: only returning to P is prohibited. To check minimality, reaching Q through its cheapest road costs 4 km. If Q is not last, that branch needs 8 km out and back; P–U plus U–R–S–T costs another 12. If Q is last, the cheapest circuit from U covering R,S,T and returning to U is 12, again giving 4 + 12 + 4 = 20. Using the 9 or 11 km roads into Q cannot improve this.'),
23:('DIV-03','Divisibility by 8 depends on the final three digits. The units digit must be 2 or 6, so test the remaining arrangements systematically. The valid complete numbers are 2536, 3256, 5632 and 6352. Each uses all four cards once and divides exactly by 8. There are four.'),
24:('GEO-05','For an a-by-b rectangle of tiles, the blue border contains 2a + 2b − 4 tiles. Setting this to 62 gives a + b = 33. The red interior has dimensions a − 2 and b − 2, whose sum is 29. For a fixed sum, the product is largest when the two whole numbers are closest: 14 and 15. Thus the maximum red count is 14 × 15 = 210.'),
25:('COUNT-06','Three distinct joining segments that connect four dots must form either a three-armed star or a path through all four dots. There are 4 stars, one for each central dot. For paths, list the four dots in order: 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24 orders, but reversing an order draws the same letter, giving 12 paths. All can be drawn continuously when retracing is allowed. Total: 4 + 12 = 16.')}
